## What a `type: shape` instance is

A first-class SHACL-style validation shape — a declarative description of what makes a memorial / tag / class instance VALID.  Per the tag-modeling ADR §2.2, shapes are inspired by the W3C SHACL standard (Shapes Constraint Language): they declare required slots, value patterns, cardinality constraints, and reference integrity checks for instances of a target class.

## Why shape-driven validation matters

The substrate's audit invariants (per `sandbar.audit.tag` and similar) currently encode validation in CODE.  Promoting validation to FIRST-CLASS SHAPE DECLARATIONS enables:

- **Validation as data** — shapes are queryable, versionable, federable
- **Per-instance shape compliance reports** — "show me the shape-validation status of this memorial"
- **Federation of constraints** — a vocabulary publisher can ship shapes alongside its concept-schemes
- **Code-free constraint authoring** — domain experts declare shapes without writing Clojure

